  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Drink Wholesome Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      A dairy-free egg white, almond and collagen protein with one to four ingredients per flavor, built for sensitive stomachs. Here is what its lab reports actually prove, what the labels say, and whether 15 g of protein a scoop justifies the price.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Lineup</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#label">What's in It?</a>
      <a href="#sweeteners">Sweeteners and Sugar</a>
      <a href="#sensitive-stomach">Sensitive Stomachs</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyers">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#compare">Vs Other Clean Brands</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Yes, Drink Wholesome is third-party tested: the brand posts lab certificates for heavy metals, gluten and glyphosate on its own site, and every result we read was below the lab's detection limit. What changes the verdict is the price and the dose, since each scoop gives only 15 g of protein at roughly $3.57 per 25 g. This review covers the test reports, every label, the sweeteners, the stomach-friendly claims and who should pay for it.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Drink Wholesome Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drink Wholesome is one of the cleanest labels in protein powder and one of the few small brands that posts its contaminant tests where anyone can read them. The unflavored egg white powder is a single ingredient, egg whites, and the flavored versions add only coconut, vanilla or cocoa, and monk fruit. The trade-off is a small 15 g dose of protein per scoop and a price well above mainstream egg white powders.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> whey, gums, sugar alcohols or stevia upset your stomach and you will pay extra for a short, transparent label. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you want 25 g or more of protein per scoop, you are allergic to egg or coconut, or you mainly care about cost per gram. For a cheaper single-ingredient egg white, see our <a href="/best-egg-white-protein-powder/">best egg white protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Per the Label at the Time of Writing</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">15 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per scoop, every flavor</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1-4</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Ingredients per flavor</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$3.57</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g protein, one-time price</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">ND</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Lead in posted lab reports (not detected)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Drink Wholesome Lineup, Label by Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drink Wholesome sells four protein powders and a meal replacement, all in 28-serving pouches at $59.99 one-time on the brand site at the time of writing. We have not tried them; the cards below are built from the brand's posted nutrition panels, its FAQ and its lab certificates. There is no whey product in the current line, so anyone who arrived expecting a Drink Wholesome whey should know the brand now positions everything as dairy-free.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is Drink Wholesome Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. Drink Wholesome's <a href="https://www.drinkwholesome.com/faq/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">FAQ</a> says it hires a third-party lab to test for gluten, heavy metals and pesticides, and its <a href="https://www.drinkwholesome.com/third-party-test-results/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Third-Party Test Results page</a> links certificates of analysis for each protein line. The copies we read show samples dated September 25, 2026 and reports finalized on October 1, 2026, covering the egg white, vegan almond, collagen and egg white plus collagen powders.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every heavy metal result on those certificates is reported as "less than" a number, which means the lab did not detect the metal above its reporting limit. The egg white and vegan almond reports list lead below 0.05 micrograms per gram, cadmium below 0.25, arsenic below 0.5 and mercury below 0.1. The newer egg white plus collagen report uses a tighter limit of 0.025 micrograms per gram for all four metals. The gluten report shows all five products below 5 ppm, and the glyphosate report shows all five below 15 ppb.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Lead limit (µg/g)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cadmium limit (µg/g)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Result</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Egg White Protein Powder</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">&lt;0.05</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">&lt;0.25</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not detected</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vegan Almond Protein Powder</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">&lt;0.05</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">&lt;0.25</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not detected</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Collagen Protein Powder</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">&lt;0.50</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">&lt;0.25</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not detected</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Egg White + Collagen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">&lt;0.025</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">&lt;0.025</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not detected</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Here is the caveat a careful reader should know. A "not detected" result is only as good as the detection limit, and some of these limits are coarse for a daily food. California's Proposition 65 sets 0.5 micrograms of lead per day as the level that requires a warning. A 21 g vanilla egg white scoop tested to below 0.05 micrograms per gram could, in arithmetic terms, hold anything up to about 1 microgram, and the 39 g chocolate almond scoop up to about 2. The reports show nothing alarming, but they do not prove a serving sits under the Prop 65 line. The egg white plus collagen report, at 0.025, gets closer to that standard.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two more limits are worth knowing. The certificates do not name the flavor tested, and chocolate flavors add cocoa, the ingredient most often blamed for lead and cadmium in protein powder. And Drink Wholesome is not NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport; its FAQ says so directly, arguing that whole-food ingredients carry little risk of banned substances. Tested athletes should treat that as a reason to pick a certified product. We found no Prop 65 warning on the product pages and no Consumer Reports or Clean Label Project result for the brand. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide explains how to read these reports.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Drink Wholesome Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
