                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Egg whites, collagen, strawberries, coconut, monk fruit</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The unflavored egg white is the purest protein in the line: 60 calories, zero fat and 15 g of protein from a 16 g scoop, which means the powder is almost entirely protein. The flavored egg whites add dried coconut for body, which is why vanilla carries 2.5 g of saturated fat and chocolate 3 g. That is not a problem for most people, but it is worth knowing if you assumed an egg white powder would be fat-free.
          </p>

          <p>
            The vegan almond powder is a different kind of product. It is made from whole, unblanched almonds rather than an isolated protein, so it brings fiber and fat with the protein. The chocolate almond panel lists 170 calories, 9 g of fat (5 g saturated, mostly from coconut), 7 g of fiber and 650 mg of potassium for the same 15 g of protein. That makes it closer to a light snack than a lean shake, and it explains why the scoop is nearly twice the size of the egg white one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sodium is the one number that surprises people. The egg white panels list 170 to 180 mg per scoop, which comes naturally from egg whites rather than added salt, while the almond versions list 0 mg. One small labeling quirk: the posted vanilla egg white panel lists only coconut in its "contains" line, although egg whites are the first ingredient. Anyone with an egg allergy should go by the ingredient list and the bag, not that line. The collagen-only powder is also 15 g of protein, but collagen lacks tryptophan, so it is not a complete protein; our <a href="/collagen-vs-protein-powder/">collagen vs protein powder</a> guide explains why that matters.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sweeteners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Does Drink Wholesome Use Stevia, Erythritol or Added Sugar?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No stevia, no erythritol and no artificial sweeteners appear on any Drink Wholesome panel we read. The sweetened protein powders use monk fruit, and the brand's FAQ says it is pure monk fruit with no erythritol blended in, which matters because many "monk fruit" sweeteners sold in stores are mostly erythritol. The unflavored egg white and unflavored almond powders contain no sweetener at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some limited-edition egg white plus collagen flavors use maple sugar instead. The Maple Cinnamon and Golden Milk panels each list 5 g of added sugar per scoop, about 10% of the daily value, while Strawberry is sweetened with monk fruit and lists 0 g added sugar. Reviews on the brand site split along this line: one buyer found a monk fruit version too sweet and another disliked the monk fruit taste, and both said they prefer the maple sugar flavors, while others want no sugar at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brand's "not our ingredients" list is long: acacia gum, guar gum, xanthan gum, carrageenan, inulin, maltodextrin, silica, soy lecithin, sucralose, acesulfame potassium, xylitol, erythritol and "natural" flavors. Several of those, especially inulin, gums and sugar alcohols, are common triggers for gas and bloating in people with IBS. Leaving them out is the main reason the powders mix less smoothly than mainstream brands, a complaint covered below.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are avoiding sweeteners entirely, buy the unflavored egg white or unflavored almond powder and sweeten with fruit. If you want a sweet shake without stevia, the monk fruit flavors are among the simplest options sold online. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-stevia/">protein powder without stevia</a> and <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">without artificial sweeteners</a> guides list alternatives at lower prices.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sensitive-stomach" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Drink Wholesome Good for a Sensitive Stomach?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For many people, probably yes, because it removes the usual suspects: lactose, gums, inulin, sugar alcohols and long additive lists. The egg white product page carries a "Low-FODMAP Certified" badge, and the FAQ says the collagen and egg white powders are low FODMAP. We could not confirm which body certified them, and the FAQ does not extend the low-FODMAP claim to the almond powder, which matters because almonds are a FODMAP food in larger servings.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brand backs the claim with a Stomach-Friendly Guarantee: if the powder upsets your stomach, it is free. Read the terms before relying on it. The guarantee is limited to the first order of each product, one multi-serving pouch, and a 30-day window, and outside of that the brand does not accept returns or issue refunds except for non-delivery or damage. The free-sample route, at $3.33 per single serving with a minimum of three, is the lower-risk way to test your own tolerance.
          </p>

          <p>
            The site features dozens of quotes from registered dietitians and buyers with IBS, GERD, SIBO, Crohn's and colitis. Those are testimonials the brand chose to display, not clinical evidence, and nobody can promise a powder will suit a given gut. Egg whites are a common allergen, coconut is labeled as a tree nut in the US, and the almond line is obviously unsuitable for nut allergies.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is not medical advice. If you have a diagnosed digestive condition, kidney disease, are pregnant or take medication, ask your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a protein powder. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-sensitive-stomach/">best protein powder for sensitive stomach</a> guide compares Drink Wholesome's approach with unflavored whey isolates, and our <a href="/best-low-fodmap-protein-powder/">low-FODMAP protein powder</a> and <a href="/best-low-histamine-protein-powder/">low-histamine protein powder</a> guides cover the narrower diets.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Much Does Drink Wholesome Cost per 25 g of Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drink Wholesome costs about $3.57 per 25 g of protein at the one-time price, which is two to three times what mainstream whey or pea proteins cost. A pouch is $59.99 for 28 servings of 15 g, so you are buying 420 g of protein. The arithmetic below uses the brand's prices at the time of writing and ignores the occasional first-order discount.
          </p>

