          <p>
            The scoop size hides some of the cost. Because each serving gives only 15 g of protein, hitting a typical 25 to 30 g post-workout dose takes nearly two scoops, so a 28-serving pouch lasts about two weeks for someone drinking one full-dose shake a day. That works out to roughly $120 a month at the one-time price.
          </p>

          <p>
            You are paying for the small-batch production, the short ingredient list and the posted lab work rather than for protein. Single-ingredient egg white powders from larger brands, the ones in our egg white guide, cost much less per gram and are also dairy-free, though they skip the guarantee and most do not post heavy metal results. If your budget is tight, the unflavored egg white plus your own fruit and cocoa is the best value in the line, since it gives the same 15 g of protein in the smallest scoop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buyers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Do Buyers Say About Drink Wholesome Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers mostly praise how Drink Wholesome sits in the stomach, and mostly complain about how it mixes. We read the 100 most recent reviews for each of the three main protein powders on the brand's own site. The egg white powder showed 76 five-star and 3 two-star reviews in that sample, the vegan almond 71 five-star and no reviews under three stars, and the egg white plus collagen 72 five-star and a single one-star review. Reviews hosted by a brand are not independent, so treat those ratios as a ceiling.
          </p>

          <p>
            The praise is consistent: buyers with IBS, reflux or lactose intolerance repeatedly say it is the first protein powder that did not bloat them, and several mention bloating by name. Chocolate and vanilla are the most mentioned flavors in positive reviews of the egg white and almond powders, and Peanut Butter and Maple Cinnamon come up most often for the egg white plus collagen line.
          </p>

          <p>
            The complaints cluster around texture and taste. Several buyers say it clumps or will not dissolve, especially in hot drinks, and one mentions too much foam. Without gums, egg white tends to foam and almond powder tends to settle, so a blender or electric frother helps. Other buyers say the vanilla tastes too much of coconut, that a monk fruit flavor is too sweet, or that one flavor was too salty. A few reported pouches arriving slashed or leaking, and one called Golden Milk "like chicken soup".
          </p>

          <p>
            Price is the quiet complaint. A buyer who rated the almond powder three stars summed it up as safe for IBS but expensive, and another asked for an organic version. Drink Wholesome's FAQ says it is not 100% organic because organic ingredients are not always available or affordable to a small business.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Drink Wholesome vs Other Clean-Label Protein Brands
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drink Wholesome's closest rivals are other short-label brands: single-ingredient egg white powders, Truvani, Equip and Owyn. Its edge is the posted lab reports and the gut-focused formulation; its weakness is the 15 g dose and the price. We have no editorial head-to-head for Drink Wholesome yet, so the comparison below is at brand level.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against plain egg white powders, Drink Wholesome wins on transparency and flavor options and loses on cost per gram and dose. Against <a href="/truvani-protein-powder-review/">Truvani</a>, it is the gentler pick for people who react to pea protein, and it posts lab results where Truvani's heavy metal story is more mixed. Against <a href="/equip-protein-powder-review/">Equip</a>, which uses beef protein isolate, it offers egg and plant options for people who avoid beef. Against <a href="/owyn-protein-powder-review/">Owyn</a>, it is far simpler, but Owyn gives 20 g or more of protein per scoop at roughly $1.55 per 25 g, per our Owyn review.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a plant powder, our sister review of <a href="/earth-chimp-protein-powder-review/">Earth Chimp</a>, published alongside this one, covers a USDA Organic pea-blend protein with 20 g a serving and probiotics. Earth Chimp is cheaper per gram and organic, but it does not publish lab results and uses agave fiber, which some people with IBS avoid. Drink Wholesome's unflavored almond powder is the simpler label of the two, while Earth Chimp gives more protein per dollar.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Drink Wholesome, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Drink Wholesome if ordinary protein powders give you gas, bloating or reflux and you have already ruled out the obvious cause, such as lactose in whey concentrate. It also suits anyone who reads labels first and wants to see heavy metal certificates before buying, and older adults or people recovering from illness who need a small, gentle protein top-up rather than a 30 g gym shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are an athlete in a drug-tested sport, because it carries no NSF or Informed Sport certification. Skip it if you are allergic to egg, coconut or almonds, if you want 25 g or more of protein in one scoop, or if you buy protein on price. Lifters on a budget will get more muscle-building protein per dollar from a whey isolate, provided their stomach tolerates it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best first purchase is three single-serving samples: unflavored egg white, vanilla egg white and chocolate almond. That costs about $10 and tells you which base your stomach and taste buds prefer before you commit to a $60 pouch.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming "not detected" means zero metals</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The posted certificates report metals below each lab's detection limit, not at zero. Some limits are coarse enough that a full serving could, arithmetically, sit above the 0.5 microgram Prop 65 lead level. The reports are reassuring, but rotate protein sources and favor unflavored over cocoa flavors if metals are your main worry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting one scoop as a full serving of protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every flavor gives 15 g of protein, roughly half to two-thirds of a typical post-workout dose. If your plan calls for 25 to 30 g, budget for two scoops, which also doubles the cost per shake and halves how long a 28-serving pouch lasts. The unflavored egg white gives that same 15 g in the smallest, cheapest scoop.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the almond powder for a low-FODMAP diet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The brand's FAQ calls the egg white and collagen powders low FODMAP, not the almond powder. Almonds contain FODMAPs in larger servings, and a 39 g scoop is a substantial portion. If you follow a low-FODMAP plan, start with the egg white line and check portions with your dietitian.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating collagen as a complete protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The Collagen Protein Powder lists 15 g of protein, but collagen lacks tryptophan and is low in leucine, so it does less for muscle than egg white or whey. Use it as a supplement to a complete protein, or choose the egg white plus collagen blend instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on the guarantee without reading the terms</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The Stomach-Friendly Guarantee covers only the first order of each product, one pouch and 30 days. After that, the brand does not take returns except for non-delivery or damage. Order single-serving samples first so a bad reaction costs a few dollars, not $60, and keep your order confirmation date handy.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Drink Wholesome third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Drink Wholesome says it hires a third-party lab to test for gluten, heavy metals and pesticides, and it posts the certificates on its Third-Party Test Results page. The October 2026 reports we read showed lead, cadmium, arsenic and mercury below detection limits for all four protein lines. It is not NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport tested for banned substances.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Drink Wholesome healthy and clean?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  By label standards it is one of the cleanest protein powders sold: one to four whole-food ingredients, no gums, no sugar alcohols, no stevia and no artificial flavors. It is not certified organic, and the flavored versions get some saturated fat from coconut. Whether it is healthy for you depends on your diet, allergies and protein needs, not the label alone.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Drink Wholesome contain dairy, whey or lactose?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. The current egg white, almond, collagen and egg white plus collagen powders contain no whey, casein or lactose, and the brand markets the whole line as dairy-free. That is why lactose-intolerant buyers use it. Check the allergen line instead: the egg powders contain egg, most flavors contain coconut, and the vegan line is made from almonds.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which Drink Wholesome flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Going by buyer reviews on the brand's site, chocolate and vanilla are the most praised flavors of the egg white and almond powders, while Peanut Butter and Maple Cinnamon are the favorites in the egg white plus collagen line. Several buyers find the vanilla too coconut-forward. We have not tasted them; buy single-serving samples to compare before choosing a pouch.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What are the most common Drink Wholesome reviews and complaints?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Buyer reviews on the brand site mostly praise that it does not cause bloating, even for people with IBS, GERD or SIBO. The common complaints are clumping or foam, especially in hot drinks, a strong coconut taste in vanilla, monk fruit sweetness, the high price, and a few pouches that arrived damaged. Those reviews are hosted by the brand, so read them as favorable.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Drink Wholesome organic?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Its FAQ says that as a small business it cannot afford to be 100% organic and uses organic ingredients when availability and price allow. The egg whites come from antibiotic- and hormone-free hens in the US, the collagen from pasture-raised cattle in Brazil and the monk fruit from China, per the brand's ingredient page. If USDA Organic is a must, look at Earth Chimp or Truvani.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Where is Drink Wholesome made?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The brand says its powders are made in Plymouth, Massachusetts, in an FDA- and USDA-inspected, GMP-certified facility, and that it manages its own supply chain. The labels list Drink Wholesome LLC in Gilford, New Hampshire, as the company the product is made for. Ingredients are sourced from several countries, which the brand lists by ingredient on its website.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

